# kanso play bugs/none_in_list.kso
#
# SHOULD: agree with itself. Writing `[1 none 3]` is refused with
#   error[none]: a list cannot hold a none: a lookup answers "not found"
#   with one, so an element would be indistinguishable
# so either a list really cannot hold a none, and map/push should refuse
# or drop one, or it can, and the literal should be allowed.
#
# DOES: prints `[1 <none> 3] 3 <none> [1 <none>] 2` on both engines. The
# lists hold nones, length counts them, and xs[2] answers a none that is
# indistinguishable from a miss -- exactly what the error says cannot
# happen. xsv/sort.kso relies on this: `numbers_at` maps fields to numbers
# or none, and the numeric comparison dispatches on the nones.
import "std/list"

fn maybe 2
  none

fn maybe n
  n

xs = list/to_list (list/map [1 2 3] maybe)
ys = push [1] none
print "{xs} {length xs} {xs[2]} {ys} {length ys}"
